WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.760
Recovery is more than turning systems back on.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:09.860
A safe recovery restores systems to a trusted state and validates that the conditions that allowed the incident are addressed.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:23.360
Depending on the case, recovery may include rebuilding hosts, restoring from backups, rotating passwords and keys, removing malicious rules, patching vulnerable services, changing access policies, and confirming that security tools are functioning.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.340
Validation matters because recovery can create a false sense of closure.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.920
Systems may appear normal while attacker persistence remains.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:38.660
Credentials may be reset while tokens, service accounts, OAuth grants, or API keys continue to provide access.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.040
Malware may be removed from one endpoint while the initial entry point remains exposed.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.340
A good recovery plan includes checks that support confidence, not just speed.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.520
Monitoring after recovery is part of the same effort.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:01:00.660
Security teams may add temporary detections, increase logging, watch for known indicators, review authentication anomalies, and monitor sensitive systems for recurrence.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.640
This period is not about panic.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.740
It is about maintaining visibility while the organization returns to normal operations.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.400
Lessons learned convert incident pain into lasting improvement.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:21.620
The post-incident review should cover root cause, timeline, detection quality, response speed, evidence availability, communication, decision points, and control gaps.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:34.080
The output should be practical: better logging, clearer escalation, improved backup testing, stronger identity controls, patched systems, better segmentation, refined detections, or updated training.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.020
The best DFIR programs make every incident a source of operational learning.
